theory.intro:
Adding $|A|$ and $|B|$ counts the overlap twice, so the **inclusion–exclusion rule** subtracts it once: $|A \cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \cap B|$. Written with $n$, this is $n(A \cup B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A \cap B)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (8,4.8);
\draw (2.9,2.4) circle (1.9);
\draw (5.1,2.4) circle (1.9);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.15,4.7) {$\xi$};
\node at (1.9,3.7) {$A$};
\node at (6.1,3.7) {$B$};
\node[font=\small] at (2.1,2.4) {$|A \cap B'|$};
\node[font=\small] at (4,2.4) {$|A \cap B|$};
\node[font=\small] at (5.9,2.4) {$|A' \cap B|$};
\node[font=\small] at (6.9,0.65) {$|(A \cup B)'|$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
For disjoint sets $|A \cap B| = 0$, so $|A \cup B| = |A| + |B|$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
